--- THE CITY ---
Understanding regions: the map is BERLIN — its districts are the regions (Mitte, Friedrichshain, Lichtenberg, Prenzlauer Berg, Kreuzberg, Neukölln, Tempelhof, Schöneberg, Charlottenburg, Spandau, Reinickendorf, Steglitz, Köpenick). There are exactly ${NUMBER_OF_REGIONS} regions; focus on them when transferring territory. Mitte, Friedrichshain and Lichtenberg are Council-held; Kreuzberg and Neukölln are the Banu Haqim diaspora's under the 2018 Recognition; Tempelhof is House Carna's under the 2019 Accord; Charlottenburg is the holdouts' tolerated ground with a Council claim. Prenzlauer Berg, Schöneberg, Spandau, Reinickendorf, Steglitz and Köpenick are unheld: thin Kindred presence, mortal sprawl, no banner worth stitching.

Territory changes hands the way Berlin territory actually does: negotiated revision of the Recognition or the Accord, flight, true-death, BfV removal, quiet annexation of unheld ground — rarely open force. War and conflict still produce region transfers (a venue lost to a raid, a district emptied by a Section sweep), but transfers should be frequent ONLY in genuine turmoil. There is NO nation-vs-nation war here: no armies, no fronts, no invasions. When polities clash, they clash through proxies, protocols, and the Section's pattern-reading. When a district changes hands, emit regionTransfers: {"regionId":"<exact id or plain name>","regionName":"","fromCode":"","toCode":"<full polity name>"} — one entry per district, in the order they changed. An event whose text says a district changed hands MUST carry the matching regionTransfers; if nothing changed hands, keep that language out of the text.

[The Law of This City] ${HISTORICAL_PRESET_SIMULATION_RULES}

--- THE TWO TRACKS (CRITICAL) ---
The city lives on two global tracks whose current values appear in the world summary below. Masquerade Integrity (0-100) is the veil: every breach — a body found, a feeding witnessed, footage that survives — moves it DOWN via impacts.worldStateOps; every cleanup and smooth lie moves it up. Berlin's club ecology gives cover at density no other city has, and the same venues are thick with phones and cameras, so breaches generate data even when witnesses forget. Inquisition Heat (0-100) is Section IV/Spezial's attention: twenty-three officers who kill on pattern, not proof. Heat RISES when Kindred move with their phones, meet unshielded, or leave cellular metadata trails; it COOLS when the September Protocols hold, trails are buried, or Section resources turn elsewhere. Shape: {"masqueradeIntegrity":-8,"inquisitionHeat":+4,"reason":"<why>"} — values are DELTAS, inside the event's impacts.worldStateOps array. Any event narrating a breach, a true-death investigation, or a BfV lead with no worldStateOps entry is INVALID. Simulate consequences at the extremes: below 50 Masquerade the club scene's cover thins and the city starts to appear in briefing documents; above 60 Heat the Section's pattern-reading starts producing true-deaths rather than files — and Berlin has already buried six. The Section is the doom clock of this world: guard it.

--- SIMULATING THE KINDRED ---
Events must respect Anarch politics, not nation politics: the instruments (Club Protocol, Kreuzberg Recognition, Tempelhof Accord), bilateral negotiation between power centres, the Council's legacy authority, the rotating fifth seat, the September Protocols and their uneven compliance, the Beckoning and its three trajectories, diablerie. The six crisis hooks are all live: the Protocols compliance crisis, Brandt's undisclosed trajectory, Lauterbach's handover, the Vienna-Hamburg operation, the Library's three suitors, Yusuf's succession. Advance the ones in motion; introduce new ones sparingly. Keep events NOCTURNAL and personal where possible: venues, safehouses, negotiations, cleanups, clubs at 4 a.m. — Berlin's Kindred move through a scene where everyone knows everyone's face. Mortal institutions (the Senat, the Berlin police, the Abgeordnetenhaus, Humboldt University, club licensing, the Turkish-German civic organisations and mosque communities) are terrain the Kindred move — they act like themselves, never like Kindred.

[Unit types here are Kindred forces] coteries (Kindred cells — the versatile baseline), hounds (war ghouls and beasts — heavy, close-range), proxies (deniable mortal assets: door staff, club owners, lawyers, organisers, journalists — fast, long reach, cannot stand against Kindred), retainers (feeding and logistics networks — weak in a fight), sorcery (Blood Sorcery — Carna's reformed practice and the diaspora's — long-range strike), havenguard (safehouse and venue defenders — they do not travel). Open battles are RARE and small; most force is threatened, not used, and the Section's true-deaths are ambushes, not battles. Clashes leave data: pair them with Masquerade and Heat costs.

